{{!-- "I WONDER..." — the running list of open questions Book I p.33 asks a GM to keep, and the
      one thing on this sheet a GM AUTHORS rather than reads. The GM playbook prints it as four
      lines of instruction beside a column of ruled ones; this is the ruled column.

      The tab's shape follows the book's own instruction, which is that the list is READ during
      play and UPDATED between sessions. So the questions come first and the reference last: a GM
      reaching for this mid-sentence wants the list, not the page about the list, which is why the
      guidance is one folded heading at the foot and starts shut.

      WRITES DO NOT GO THROUGH THE FORM. Every field here is handled by gm-wonder-tab.js and saved
      straight to `actor.system.wonders`, so none of them carries a `name` attribute: a `name` on
      an input inside this sheet's <form> puts it in core's submit data, where the array indices
      would expand into an OBJECT keyed "0", "1", ... and quietly replace the list with it.

      The two text fields save on BLUR without re-rendering, and the three buttons re-render. That
      split, and why it has to be a split, is the header of gm-wonder-tab.js.

      NO ROW HERE CARRIES A BULLET IN ITS MARKUP. The question-spiral beside every question — the
      add bar's, an open entry's, a settled one's — is a `::before` on the row, so there is no
      element to look for and nothing to keep in step across the three. stonetop.css says why it
      is drawn rather than printed, and where the gutter it hangs in comes from.

      `data-tab="wonder"` earns the rail's question-mark glyph; the icon table in stonetop.css is
      one flat list keyed by data-tab with no per-sheet scope.

      The three headings below use the default `stonetop-move-group-title` stem, which is half of
      HEADING_SELECTOR in StonetopGmToolkitSheet.js. The fold walk claims a heading's FOLLOWING
      SIBLINGS until the next heading — which the section boxes below now bound anyway, so a
      section may hold as many siblings after its heading as it likes. What it still must not do
      is put a SECOND heading in a box: the first caret would stop claiming at it.

      A PENCIL PER SECTION, in each one's own top-right corner, which is where every other
      section in the system keeps its. That is what boxes the two lists: a pencil is positioned
      against the section it belongs to, so a section has to BE something. The boxes pay for
      themselves in the fold too — `_sectionFoldTargets` reads a corner caret's siblings for the
      heading and then walks that heading's own siblings, so it wants a box around each pair
      exactly as the Threats tab's `.steading-threats-section` gives it one.

      Each pencil guards the three structural buttons of ITS list and nothing else: the tick that
      files a question, the arrow that puts one back, and the trash. Everything else stays live
      while both are off, which is the point rather than an oversight — the reason this list
      exists is to be added to and answered in mid-sentence, and a tab that has to be unlocked
      first is one nobody writes on. What the pencils are for is the pair of one-click buttons at
      the end of every row: mid-session, with a dozen rows on screen, the tick and the trash are
      two pixels apart from a stray click's point of view.

      The guidance section is boxed like the other two but carries no pencil, for the reason the
      Threats tab's reference sections carry none: there is nothing in it to edit.

      NOT `stonetop-readonly`. That class is the sheet-wide lock (pointer-events off every input,
      textarea and button, then an allow-list), and on this tab it would put out exactly the
      fields the paragraph above says have to stay lit. `--reading` hides the one span it is
      about and touches nothing else. --}}
<div class="tab wonder" data-group="primary" data-tab="wonder">
	<section class="sheet-tab stonetop-gm-wonder">

		<p class="steading-threats-intro">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.intro"}}</p>

		{{!-- The add bar sits ABOVE the list rather than at its foot, unlike the prep tabs' "write
		      one up" bars. Those open a dialog and are the end of a long act; this one is a line
		      of typing, most often done while the list is already long, and a bar that walks down
		      the page as the list grows is one a GM has to go looking for. --}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-wonder-add">
			<input type="text" class="stonetop-gm-wonder-new" maxlength="300"
			       aria-label="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.add"}}"
			       placeholder="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.addPlaceholder"}}">
			<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-wonder-add-btn"
			        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.add"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
				<i class="fas fa-plus"></i> {{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.addShort"}}
			</button>
		</div>

		{{!-- The pencil goes FIRST, ahead of the heading, which is not a style choice: the fold
		      walk finds a corner caret's heading with `caret.parentElement.querySelector(...)` and
		      then claims that heading's following siblings, so a caret emitted after the list
		      would leave the list behind it with nothing to claim. The shared toggle emits the
		      pencil and the caret as one pair, which is why they travel together.

		      No fold caret on this one: the open list is what the tab is FOR, and a folded one is
		      a tab that opens on nothing. --}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-wonder-section steading-edit-section{{#unless stonetop.edit.wonderOpen}} stonetop-gm-wonder-section--reading{{/unless}}">
		{{> "stonetop.steading-section-toggle" section="wonderOpen" editing=stonetop.edit.wonderOpen}}
		{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.open")
		    note=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.openNote")}}

		{{#if stonetop.wonders.open.length}}
		<ul class="stonetop-gm-wonder-list">
			{{#each stonetop.wonders.open}}
			<li class="stonetop-gm-wonder-item" data-wonder-id="{{id}}">
				<div class="stonetop-gm-wonder-head">
					{{!-- The question itself is the input, not a label with a pencil beside it.
					      It is one line of text that gets reworded as often as it gets answered
					      ("maybe the question isn't how do the bandits keep the ghosts at bay,
					      but which of the bandits have been possessed" — Book I p.418), and a
					      dialog for a one-line edit is a dialog nobody opens. --}}
					<input type="text" class="stonetop-gm-wonder-question" value="{{question}}" maxlength="300"
					       aria-label="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.questionLabel"}}">
					<span class="stonetop-gm-wonder-tools">
						<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-wonder-settle"
						        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.settle"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
							<i class="fas fa-check"></i>
						</button>
						<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-wonder-remove"
						        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.remove"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
							<i class="fas fa-trash"></i>
						</button>
					</span>
				</div>
				{{!-- Always present, never behind a disclosure. Half of what the book asks a GM to
				      do with this list is to answer PART of a question, or to note the hunch that
				      is not yet an answer, and a box that has to be opened first is one that only
				      gets used when the answer is already final. --}}
				<textarea class="stonetop-gm-wonder-answer" rows="2"
				          aria-label="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.answerLabel"}}"
				          placeholder="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.answerPlaceholder"}}">{{answer}}</textarea>
			</li>
			{{/each}}
		</ul>
		{{else}}
		<p class="stonetop-gm-wonder-empty">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.empty"}}</p>
		{{/if}}
		</div>

		{{!-- The book says to REMOVE a question once it has been answered. The tick does the
		      softer half of that: it takes the question off the list a GM reads mid-session, and
		      keeps the answer, which is now a decision about the world that the rest of the prep
		      may rest on. The trash still deletes. Rendered only when there is something in it, so
		      an untouched tab shows one list and not two.

		      It carries a fold caret but starts OPEN, like every other section on this sheet: a
		      section shut on first open is one a GM never learns is there. Folding it is a choice
		      the caret offers and the sheet then remembers per user.

		      The caret is handed to the TOGGLE rather than to the heading, unlike the guidance
		      section below: with a pencil in the corner the two belong side by side there, which
		      is how the steading's cards carry the pair and what
		      `.steading-edit-section:has(> .stonetop-section-collapse)` steps the pencil left
		      for. Left on the heading it would sit inline in the title while the pencil floated
		      above it. --}}
		{{#if stonetop.wonders.settled.length}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-wonder-section steading-edit-section{{#unless stonetop.edit.wonderSettled}} stonetop-gm-wonder-section--reading{{/unless}}">
		{{> "stonetop.steading-section-toggle" section="wonderSettled" editing=stonetop.edit.wonderSettled
		    collapse="gm-wonder-settled"}}
		{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.settled")
		    note=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.settledNote")}}
		<ul class="stonetop-gm-wonder-list stonetop-gm-wonder-list--settled">
			{{#each stonetop.wonders.settled}}
			<li class="stonetop-gm-wonder-item" data-wonder-id="{{id}}">
				<div class="stonetop-gm-wonder-head">
					<span class="stonetop-gm-wonder-question stonetop-gm-wonder-question--settled">{{question}}</span>
					<span class="stonetop-gm-wonder-tools">
						<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-wonder-reopen"
						        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.reopen"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
							<i class="fas fa-rotate-left"></i>
						</button>
						<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-wonder-remove"
						        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.remove"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
							<i class="fas fa-trash"></i>
						</button>
					</span>
				</div>
				{{#if answer}}<p class="stonetop-gm-wonder-answer-text">{{answer}}</p>{{/if}}
			</li>
			{{/each}}
		</ul>
		</div>
		{{/if}}

		{{!-- Book I p.33, which is the page the playbook's four lines are a summary of. Reference
		      rather than content, but it still opens EXPANDED — the same treatment the Threats tab
		      gives its prep reference.

		      NO PENCIL, so its caret stays on the heading where `section-heading` puts it: the
		      corner placement above is what a pencil brings with it, and a lone caret floating in
		      an otherwise empty corner would read as a control that had lost its pair. --}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-wonder-section">
		{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.guide")
		    note=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.wonder.guideNote")
		    collapse="gm-wonder-guide"}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-wonder-guide">
			{{#each stonetop.wonderGuide}}
			<div class="stonetop-gm-wonder-guide-section">
				<h4 class="stonetop-gm-wonder-guide-title">{{name}}</h4>
				<ul class="stonetop-gm-wonder-guide-items">
					{{#each items}}<li>{{this}}</li>{{/each}}
				</ul>
			</div>
			{{/each}}
		</div>
		</div>

	</section>
</div>
